SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The existing Role-based Access Control (RBAC) system manages user permissions in a multi-user environment by assigning roles and maintaining a role hierarchy. Users have security clearance levels (High, Medium, Low), which regulate their ability to hold roles, submit requests, and delegate roles. Requests involve actions on system objects classified by security level, and are processed concurrently in FIFO order, ensuring authorization correctness, consistency of delegation and permissions, and system safety and liveness. Higher-clearance users can delegate roles temporarily to lower-clearance users, with delegation subject to clearance constraints and revocable either after fixed expiration or by administrators.

The prospective Emergency Module extends this system by introducing an Emergency mode activated by administrator consensus during Normal mode, changing request processing order and role constraints temporarily, and then reverting the system back to Normal mode with cleanup actions.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS
============================================
- Users possess a security clearance level: High, Medium, or Low.
- Administrators are users with High clearance, but not all High-clearance users are administrators.
- Users hold assigned roles or delegated roles, constrained by clearance levels.
- Requests affect system objects, each with a classification level (High, Medium, Low).
- Request classification is the highest classification among affected objects.
- Users may only submit requests with classification levels ≤ their clearance level.
- Higher-clearance users may approve or reject requests from lower-clearance users.
- Requests are processed concurrently in FIFO order.
- Every request is eventually processed (liveness).
- Higher-clearance users may delegate roles temporarily to lower-clearance users if the delegate meets the role’s minimum clearance.
- Delegated roles expire after a fixed time or can be revoked by administrators at any time.
- The system must preserve authorization correctness, delegation and permission consistency, safety, and liveness under concurrency.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
=======================
R1: Emergency Mode Triggering  
While in Normal mode, exactly two administrators must jointly trigger an emergency to cause the system to enter Emergency mode.

R2: Emergency Mode Duration  
Emergency mode is active only for a limited (finite) time, after which the system must return to Normal mode.

R3: Emergency Mode Behavior  
During Emergency mode:  
  R3.1: Request processing may bypass FIFO order, allowing prioritization of multiple high classification requests concurrently.  
  R3.2: Users may temporarily hold mutually exclusive roles simultaneously.  
  R3.3: Administrators temporarily obtain emergency privileges.

R4: Post-Emergency Mode Cleanup  
After Emergency mode ends:  
  R4.1: Emergency privileges granted to administrators must be revoked.  
  R4.2: Roles delegated during the emergency must be revoked.  
  R4.3: Administrators must update their credentials.

KEY CONCEPTS AND ENTITIES
==========================
- User: An entity with a security clearance level (High, Medium, Low), who may be an administrator or standard user, and holds assigned or delegated roles.  
- Administrator: A user with High clearance who can trigger emergencies, revoke delegations, and update credentials.  
- Role: A set of permissions; roles have minimum clearance requirements; roles can be assigned or delegated to users.  
- Delegated Role: A role temporarily assigned to a user by a higher-clearance user, subject to expiration or revocation.  
- Request: An action submitted by a user, affecting one or more objects, with a classification level equal to the highest classification of affected objects.  
- Object: A system entity with a classification level (High, Medium, Low).  
- Clearance Level: Security level assigned to users (High, Medium, Low) determining permissions and role eligibility.  
- Classification Level: Security level assigned to system objects and requests.  
- Emergency Mode: A special system state triggered by exactly two administrators, lasting for a limited time, modifying request processing and role privileges.  
- Emergency Privileges: Temporary privileges granted to administrators during Emergency mode.  
- Mutually Exclusive Roles: Roles that normally cannot be held simultaneously by a user, but may be held temporarily during Emergency mode.  
- Normal Mode: The standard system operating mode outside of Emergency mode.

ASSUMPTIONS REQUIRING CLARIFICATION
====================================
1. What is the specific duration or criterion for the "limited time" that Emergency mode remains active before returning to Normal mode? Is it fixed, configurable, or event-driven?  
2. Are the "exactly 2 administrators" who trigger the emergency required to act simultaneously or is it sufficient that two distinct administrators trigger it within some time window?  
3. Are the mutually exclusive roles that can be held simultaneously during Emergency mode strictly limited to certain roles, or does this apply to all mutually exclusive roles?  
4. Does "administrators must update their credentials" after Emergency mode imply mandatory credential changes for all administrators, or only those involved in the emergency?  
5. Should the revocation of delegated roles after Emergency mode (R4.2) apply only to delegations made during Emergency mode, or also existing delegations from Normal mode?

CONSTRAINTS
===========
- Clearance levels and classification levels are ordered as High > Medium > Low.  
- Users may only submit requests with classification ≤ their clearance level.  
- Delegation of roles must respect minimum clearance requirements of the role.  
- Emergency mode can only be triggered from Normal mode by exactly two administrators.  
- Emergency mode duration is finite and must eventually revert to Normal mode.  
- During Emergency mode, FIFO request processing order may be overridden for prioritization of high classification requests.  
- Mutual exclusivity of roles is relaxed only during Emergency mode.  
- Emergency privileges granted during Emergency mode must be revoked upon return to Normal mode.  
- Delegated roles granted during Emergency mode must be revoked after Emergency mode ends.  
- Administrators must update credentials after Emergency mode ends.  
- Requests are processed concurrently, with liveness guarantees in both Normal and Emergency modes.
```

